{
  /*
    The preload, type-checked as what it is: a script that runs in a renderer.

    It lived in `tsconfig.node.json` alongside the main process, which has `lib: ["ES2023"]` and no DOM
    — deliberately, so a main-process module cannot quietly start using `document` and still compile.
    The preload paid for that with a narrowed `globalThis` cast every time it needed `location`, which
    was tolerable for two reads of a hostname and stopped being tolerable the moment a file needed
    `MutationObserver`, `HTMLStyleElement` and a `<style>` element: the casts became longer than the
    code and gave up every type they were standing in for.

    A separate project keeps both properties. The preload gets real DOM types; the main process still
    has none, so the rule that mattered is unchanged rather than relaxed.

    Electron's own types come with it, because a preload is the one place that legitimately speaks both
    `ipcRenderer` and `document`.
  */
  "extends": "./tsconfig.base.json",
  "include": ["src/preload/**/*", "src/shared/**/*"],
  "compilerOptions": {
    "lib": ["ES2023", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "types": ["node"],
    "tsBuildInfoFile": "node_modules/.tmp/tsconfig.preload.tsbuildinfo",
    "baseUrl": ".",
    "paths": {
      "@shared/*": ["src/shared/*"]
    }
  }
}
